Sec. 40001 adds a new Subchapter V to title 14 of the U.S. Code, appropriating $24,593,500,000 to the Coast Guard for fiscal year 2025 (available through September 30, 2029) to procure new assets and improve shore infrastructure. The funds are allocated across 13 categories, including $4.3 billion each for Offshore Patrol Cutters and Polar Security Cutters, $3.5 billion for Arctic Security Cutters, $2.283 billion for rotary-wing aircraft, $1.1425 billion for fixed-wing aircraft, and $4.379 billion for shore facilities (with sub-allocations such as $500 million for the Coast Guard Yard and $425 million for enlisted boot camp barracks). Smaller amounts include $816 million for icebreaking cutters, $1 billion for Fast Response Cutters, $266 million for long-range unmanned aircraft, $170 million for maritime domain awareness, and $75 million for autonomous maritime systems. The section also makes a technical conforming amendment to the chapter's table of sections.

Sec. 40002 reauthorizes the FCC's spectrum auction authority through September 30, 2034 (excluding the 3.1–3.45 GHz and 7.4–8.4 GHz bands), and requires the Commission to auction at least 300 MHz of spectrum, including at least 100 MHz in the 3.98–4.2 GHz band within 2 years. It directs the Assistant Secretary of Commerce to identify 500 MHz of federal spectrum in the 1.3–10.5 GHz covered band for reallocation (at least 200 MHz within 2 years, the remainder within 4 years), with auctions scheduled within 4 and 8 years, and allows the President to modify or withdraw frequencies 60 days before an auction for national security. It appropriates $50 million to the Department of Commerce through September 30, 2034, for spectrum analysis of the 2.7–2.9 GHz, 4.4–4.9 GHz, and 7.25–7.4 GHz bands and biennial reports on federal spectrum value.

Sec. 40003 appropriates funds to the FAA Administrator for fiscal year 2025 (available through September 30, 2029) to improve aviation safety, including $4.75 billion for telecommunications infrastructure modernization, $3 billion for radar systems replacement, $1.9 billion to construct a new air route traffic control center (with at least 3 existing ARTCCs divested), $1 billion for TRACON recapitalization, and $500 million for runway safety technologies. Additional amounts include $300 million each for Enterprise Information Display Systems and for section 619 of the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024, $350 million for unstaffed infrastructure sustainment, $100 million each for ARTCC realignment (closing or consolidating at least 10 ARTCCs) and air traffic controller advanced training technologies, and $80 million for at least 50 Automated Weather Observing Systems, 60 Visual Weather Observing Systems, and 64 weather camera sites. The FAA Administrator must submit quarterly reports to Congress on expenditures beginning 180 days after enactment.

Sec. 40004 adds section 50924 to title 51, requiring the Secretary of Transportation to impose a per-launch or reentry fee on commercial space launches and reentries licensed under section 50904 beginning in 2026, equal to the lesser of a per-pound payload rate (starting at $0.25 in 2026 and rising to $1.50 by 2033, then indexed to CPI) or a flat cap (starting at $30,000 in 2026 and rising to $200,000 by 2033, then indexed). Fees are deposited into a new Office of Commercial Space Transportation Launch and Reentry Licensing and Permitting Fund, with 70 percent available to the Office of Commercial Space Transportation and for section 630(b) of the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 without further appropriation. A clerical amendment adds the new section to the table of sections.

Sec. 40005 adds section 20306 to title 51, appropriating $9,995,000,000 to NASA for fiscal year 2025 (available through September 30, 2032) for Mars, Artemis, and Moon to Mars activities. Allocations include $700 million for a competitively bid, firm fixed-price Mars telecommunications orbiter (delivered by December 31, 2028), $2.6 billion for the Gateway program (at least $750 million obligated each in FY2026–2028), $4.1 billion for Space Launch System expenses for Artemis IV and V (at least $1.025 billion each in FY2026–2029), $1.25 billion for International Space Station operations, and $1 billion for infrastructure improvements at NASA manned spaceflight centers (with specified amounts for Stennis, Kennedy, Johnson, Marshall, and Michoud). It also provides $325 million to fulfill contract 80JSC024CA002 and directs transfer of a flown space vehicle to a field center for public exhibition, with at least 50 percent of funds obligated by September 30, 2028, 100 percent by September 30, 2029, and all outlays by September 30, 2034.

Sec. 40006 amends section 32912 of title 49 to set corporate average fuel economy civil penalties at $0.00 (striking the prior $5 and $10 figures), effective on enactment and applicable to all model years for which the Secretary of Transportation has not issued a penalty notification under section 32903(b)(2)(B).

Sec. 40007 amends section 49104(b) of title 49 to require the Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority to pay the Treasury annually $3,000,000 in 1987 dollars (adjusted by the GNP Price Deflator) from 1987 through 2026, and $15,000,000 in 2027 dollars for 2027 and subsequent years. The Secretary and the Airports Authority must renegotiate lease payments at least once every 10 years to ensure no year's payment falls below $15,000,000 in 2027 dollars.

Sec. 40008 rescinds all unobligated balances appropriated or made available by sections 40001, 40002, 40003, and 40004 of Public Law 117-169 (the Inflation Reduction Act).

Sec. 40009 amends section (d)(2)(B) of the Travel Promotion Act of 2009 to reduce the annual transfer to the Travel Promotion Fund from $100,000,000 to $20,000,000.

Sec. 40010 rescinds any unobligated balances made available by section 40007(a) of title IV of Public Law 117-169 for alternative fuel and low-emission aviation technology.

Sec. 40011 permanently rescinds $850,000,000 of the unobligated balances made available under section 106(a) of the CHIPS Act of 2022 for the Public Wireless Supply Chain Innovation Fund. [end of text]


